{"findings":[{"blocking":false,"classId":"rubric-partial-ac-coverage","classStatement":"A rubric row's pass condition tests only part of the behavior its acceptance criterion states.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC2 says \"A missing, empty, wrongly typed or out-of-bounds field ... is refused naming its path\", but R2 only tests missing, empty and over-bound text fields, empty or oversized sites, and a bad path or line. Nothing tests a wrongly typed text field (for example class: 42) or a sites value that is not a list.","failureScenario":"Implementer only checks string length -> a numeric `class` passes validation and the tests -> R2 still PASSes while part of AC2 is unverified.","id":"F1","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"undefined-dedup-identity","classStatement":"A deduplication requirement does not state what makes two items the same.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC3 says records are carried \"without duplicates\" but does not say what makes two records duplicates. In a paired round, both peers' inputs hold the same records. A finding claimed fixed again in a later round produces a second record with the same findingId but a different round or content. R3 tests neither case.","failureScenario":"Implementer dedupes by findingId only -> a second sweep of the same finding in a later round is silently dropped from later packets, or no dedupe is done and paired-round records appear twice. Neither is caught by R3.","id":"F2","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"rubric-partial-ac-coverage","classStatement":"A rubric row's pass condition tests only part of the behavior its acceptance criterion states.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC6 says a peer's \"`resolved` or `unsupported` in that round does not close it\", but R6 tests only the #134 scenario, which uses `resolved`.","failureScenario":"Prepass fix handles `resolved` but not `unsupported` -> a peer's `unsupported` still clears a re-reported regression's origin -> R6 PASSes anyway.","id":"F3","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"unspecified-null-ordering","classStatement":"A nullable value is used in an ordering comparison without a defined rule for null.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC3 allows a record's `round` to be null \"outside the round policy\". AC4 compares records \"from a round no later than the one being qualified\" but does not say how a null round compares.","failureScenario":"A record with a null round ends up in an open scope -> the implementation treats null as 0 (or as always later) -> it inconsistently includes or excludes the record from the regression check.","id":"F4","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"stale-artifact-metadata","classStatement":"Artifact status or cross-references do not match the recorded approval state.","document":"requirements/design.md","evidence":"The design's status line still says \"**Status:** draft\", but scratchpad S2 records that `approve-design` was given. The design and spec also cite interview D1-D8, while only D1-D7 were supplied for assessment.","failureScenario":"A later reader checks the design's status -> sees draft and questions whether implementation was authorized against an approved design.","id":"F5","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"mandate-reporting-step-unplanned","classStatement":"A reporting duty the mandate requires has no step in the plan.","document":"requirements/plan.md","evidence":"The mandate requires open choices, including folding in #134, to be called out in the PR description. The plan's Strategy, Steps and Verification sections never mention the PR description.","failureScenario":"The PR is opened without noting that #134 was folded in, or without listing the D1-D7 choices -> the human has to rediscover them during review.","id":"F6","severity":"low"}],"mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"Goal: the spec targets #144 AC4 sweep records plus cross-round regression tracking. AC4 makes a missed site of a swept class a regression, and the plan is one slice and one PR with no merge step. #134 is folded in (D6, AC6) with a stated reason: it is the same regression-origin class and sits in the same qualification path. Round policy and disposition records: most of the work lives in the new sweep-records.js, in review-findings.js and in boundary-review.js `roundSchedule`. The execution.js edits are limited to the validation call, packet fields, roundPolicy.revision and one instruction sentence, and local-agent-adapters.js is untouched, which leaves room for the parallel #142/#143 work. Correction policy: AC4 states \"GateReeve never changes a reviewer's `blocking` flag\"; reviewers are guided, not overridden. Extensibility for #148: D1 allows more disposition values, and the design reserves new optional record fields under a new format version (`sweep-record/1`). The agent decisions D1-D7 and S2 are recorded with alternatives, can be reversed, and none of them is an irreversible choice the human would need to make. No public text is produced at this stage.","outcome":"PASS"},"outcome":"PASS","summary":"The spec can go to implementation; none of the findings block. Each of AC1-AC7 is observable and testable, and each matches the design's chosen shape (D1-D6): the claim disposition, the bounded sweep record with field-path errors, records carried through the open review scope, the swept-class regression rule that never changes a reviewer's blocking flag, the reviewer guide, the #134 prepass and grouping fix, and the docs plus the byte-identical mirror. Rubric rows R1-R7 each have binary pass and fail conditions and name their evidence. The plan steps P1-P6 and issues I-1 to I-4 cover every rubric row and add nothing beyond the design. All seven agent-answered decisions (D1-D7, S2) stay within the mandate, can be reversed and are recorded. The six findings are all low and non-blocking: test gaps and ambiguities at the edges, plus some housekeeping."}